---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import Track from "../components/Track.astro";

const description =
  "Answers about dj-digger: what it is, what it costs, which computers and browsers it runs on, what it does with SoundCloud, download gates and store carts, rekordbox, and your data.";
---

<Base title="dj-digger questions and answers" description={description} label="about">
  <Track id="faq" title="Questions and answers" hero sub>
    <Fragment slot="heading">Questions <span class="grad">and answers</span></Fragment>
    <p class="lede">What dj-digger does, what it leaves to you, and what it does not do.</p>
  </Track>

  <Track id="basics" title="The app">
    <div class="stack">
      <h3>What is dj-digger?</h3>
      <p class="copy">
        dj-digger is a free, open-source crate-digging app for DJs on Windows, macOS and Linux, with a browser
        extension. Paste a SoundCloud playlist, likes, reposts or a profile and it imports every track into a library
        you can audition. You mark each track got or skipped, and it finds where to buy it (Bandcamp, Beatport,
        Traxsource, Juno Download, Apple, record shops) or how to download it free.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack">
      <h3>Is it free?</h3>
      <p class="copy">
        Yes. dj-digger is open source under the Apache License 2.0, with no account, no subscription and no ads. The
        tracks you buy are paid for in the stores, by you.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack">
      <h3>Which computers and browsers does it run on?</h3>
      <p class="copy">
        Windows 10 (version 1809 or newer) and 11, macOS 15 or newer on Apple Silicon and Intel, and Linux. The
        extension runs in Chrome, Edge and Brave; browsers installed as Flatpak or Snap cannot connect to the app.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack">
      <h3>Does it know which tracks I already own?</h3>
      <p class="copy">
        It scans the music folders you choose. A confident artist-and-title match marks the track got and plays it
        from disk, and that mark follows the track into every playlist it appears in.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Track>

  <Track id="files" title="Downloads and buying">
    <div class="stack">
      <h3>Does it rip music from SoundCloud?</h3>
      <p class="copy">
        No. It reads playlists through SoundCloud's API, and previews are decoded in memory, never saved. Files come
        only from routes the artist offers: a SoundCloud download the artist enabled, the download gate the artist set
        up, or a store where you buy the track.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack">
      <h3>What does it do with download gates such as Hypeddit?</h3>
      <p class="copy">
        Gates on Hypeddit, ToneDen, GateRush and Droploud are completed through their own download flows. When a gate
        needs the browser, the extension walks its steps and the file arrives in the app. Social steps follow
        <em>Allow social actions</em> in Settings; CAPTCHAs, sign-ins and first approvals stay with you.
      </p>
      <p class="copy"><a href="/guides/hypeddit-download-gates/">Hypeddit download gates in 2026</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack">
      <h3>Does it buy tracks for me?</h3>
      <p class="copy">
        It fills your Bandcamp cart, and your Beatport cart when the exact track is known, in your own browser. Every
        product page is checked again right before it adds a track. It never reads your password, picks a payment
        method or checks out.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Track>

  <Track id="decks" title="DJ software and decks">
    <div class="stack">
      <h3>Does it work with rekordbox, Serato or Traktor?</h3>
      <p class="copy">
        It works beside them. dj-digger never writes rekordbox data or audio tags. It exports copies of your files in a
        format the decks you tick can play, FLAC or WAV, into a new folder that your DJ software can import like any
        other.
      </p>
      <p class="copy"><a href="/guides/soundcloud-to-rekordbox/">From a SoundCloud playlist to rekordbox</a></p>
    </div>
  </Track>

  <Track id="privacy" title="Your data">
    <div class="stack">
      <h3>Where does my data go?</h3>
      <p class="copy">
        Your library, decisions and preferences stay on your computer: there is no dj-digger server. The extension
        talks only to the app on the same computer, and nothing is sent to the developer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Track>

  <Track id="get" title="Download" heading="Download dj-digger">
    <div class="cta"><a class="button primary" href="/download/"><Icon name="download-simple" />Download</a></div>
  </Track>
</Base>
